Summary
-------
PortReveal is free for non-commercial internal use. It is not open source.
Commercial use requires a separate written agreement with the copyright holder.

1. Grant (non-commercial internal use)

You may install, run, copy, and modify PortReveal solely for non-commercial
internal operations of your organization (or for personal non-commercial use),
including evaluating it and operating it on networks you are authorized to manage.

2. Restrictions

Without prior written permission from the copyright holder, you may not:

- Use PortReveal for commercial purposes (including selling, renting,
  hosting as a paid service, bundling into a commercial product, or using it
  as a material part of a revenue-generating offering)
- Redistribute PortReveal, modified or unmodified, to third parties
- Sublicense, sell, or publicly re-publish the source as if it were open source
- Remove or alter this license notice

3. No open-source license

No OSI-approved or similar open-source license is granted. Publication of this
repository (for example on GitHub) does not change these terms.

4. Third-party software

PortReveal depends on separate tools and libraries that remain under their
own licenses. Installing or using those components does not grant you rights
beyond each component’s license. Notable runtime tools include:

- Nmap - Nmap Public Source License (NPSL); see https://nmap.org/npsl/
- Npcap (Windows packet capture) - Nmap/Npc license; commercial use may
  require a separate Npcap OEM/commercial license from Nmap Software LLC
- PostgreSQL - PostgreSQL License
- Playwright / Chromium - Apache-2.0 and Chromium/BSD-related terms
- FFmpeg - LGPL/GPL (depending on build)
- FreeRDP - Apache-2.0
- Other Node.js and Python packages - see each package’s license metadata

You are responsible for complying with those third-party licenses, including
any commercial restrictions they impose (especially Nmap and Npcap).

